From: crackerjacques <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: sprd: step the non-interleaved DMA by the sample width

sprd_pcm_dma_step_config() derives the DMA address step from the sample
width for interleaved access, but hard-codes two bytes for non-interleaved:

  step = (dma_data->use_mcdt == 1) ?
  	DMA_SLAVE_BUSWIDTH_4_BYTES : DMA_SLAVE_BUSWIDTH_2_BYTES;

Both widths do reach this path. sprd-fe-dai.c picks the datawidth from the
format, DMA_SLAVE_BUSWIDTH_4_BYTES for S24_LE and _2_BYTES otherwise, and
the platform advertises S16_LE, S24_LE and S32_LE together with
SNDRV_PCM_INFO_NONINTERLEAVED. So an S24_LE non-interleaved stream walks a
four-byte-per-sample buffer two bytes at a time: every frame is fed to the
VBC FIFO twice, once as its low half and once as its high half, and the
stream runs at half speed with the sample noise on top of it.

That is what a stock PipeWire session negotiates on RG Rotate - it opens
hw:0,0 as MMAP_NONINTERLEAVED / S24_LE - and it is why playback there is
loud broadband crackle while speaker-test, which takes RW_INTERLEAVED /
S16_LE, is clean on the same card.

Use desc->datawidth, which for the two-byte case is the value that was
already being written.

Signed-off-by: crackerjacques <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
---
diff --git a/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.c b/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.c
index 2bce85075ae1..8f49e096ba44 100755
--- a/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.c
+++ b/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.c
@@ -835,8 +835,8 @@ static int sprd_pcm_dma_step_config(struct sprd_pcm_dma_params *dma_data,
 		       desc->datawidth);
 	} else {
 		sp_asoc_pr_dbg("Non-interleaved Access\n");
 		step = (dma_data->use_mcdt == 1) ?
-			DMA_SLAVE_BUSWIDTH_4_BYTES : DMA_SLAVE_BUSWIDTH_2_BYTES;
+			DMA_SLAVE_BUSWIDTH_4_BYTES : desc->datawidth;
 		desc->src_step = playback ? step : 0;
 		desc->des_step = playback ? 0 : step;
 		sp_asoc_pr_info("desc->src_step=%d, dest_step=%d\n",
